{"name":"Teary Eyed","hex":"#ded2e8","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"teary-eyed","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having tears in one's eyes, as if from crying."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/teary-eyed"}],"entries":[{"title":"Teary Eyed","blocks":[{"text":"\"Teary Eyed\" a song by American recording artist Missy Elliott. It was written by Elliott and Warryn Campbell for her sixth studio album, The Cookbook (2005), while production was handled by the latter. Released as the album's second single on September 12, 2005, following \"Lose Control\" (2005), the song reached the top 50 in Australia, Ireland, Switzerland, and the United Kingdom"}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/52/Missyelliott_tearyeyed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teary_Eyed","wikidata":"Q3504723"},{"title":"Tears","blocks":[{"text":"Tears are a transparent fluid secreted primarily by the lacrimal glands found in the eyes of all land mammals. According to the mode of production, tears are classified into four types: basal, closed eye, emotional, and reflex. The basal rate of tear secretion is ~0.5–2.2 μL/min, and irritation can increase secretion by up to ~100-fold, reaching ~300 μL/min. Tears are made up of water, electrolytes, proteins, lipids, and mucins that form layers on the surface of eyes. The four types of tears differ significantly in their composition."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/USMC-04952.jpg/330px-USMC-04952.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tears","wikidata":"Q76469"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/52/Missyelliott_tearyeyed.jpg/250px-Missyelliott_tearyeyed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/52/Missyelliott_tearyeyed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/52/Missyelliott_tearyeyed.jpg/250px-Missyelliott_tearyeyed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMissyelliott_tearyeyed.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/USMC-04952.jpg/500px-USMC-04952.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/USMC-04952.jpg/1280px-USMC-04952.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/USMC-04952.jpg/500px-USMC-04952.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Human tears","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUSMC-04952.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Crying-girl.jpg/500px-Crying-girl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Crying-girl.jpg/1280px-Crying-girl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Crying-girl.jpg/500px-Crying-girl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A toddler producing tears due to emotional stress or pain","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrying-girl.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Crying_boy.jpg/500px-Crying_boy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Crying_boy.jpg/500px-Crying_boy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","al